# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the Salam compiler's leak gate
# (c/tools/bash/leakcheck.sh, .github/workflows/lint-memory-leaks.yml).
#
# Format: one `leak:<substring>` per line. LSan matches the substring
# against every frame of the allocation stack, so a match here means "this
# allocation is not the compiler's to free".
#
# Keep this file SMALL and justify every entry. A suppression hides a real
# LSan finding forever; the reflex when the gate goes red should be to fix
# the leak, not to add a line here.

# The tree-walking interpreter's `extern` bridge (interp_ffi.c). Every
# nf_malloc/nf_realloc that reaches here was requested by the Salam program
# being interpreted - `salam exec prog.salam` running a prog.salam that calls
# std/mem.Allocate, directly or through a stdlib function that returns an
# owned string. Salam is manually memory-managed, so whether that block is
# freed is the interpreted program's business, exactly as it would be in a
# compiled build; the interpreter must not free it on the program's behalf
# (it has no idea when the program is done with it) and it does not leak a
# single byte of its own here. tests/en/exec/strings.salam is the standing
# example: it prints str.Title(...)/str.Reverse(...)/str.Replace(...), each
# of which returns a fresh buffer the test then drops.
leak:call_native_extern

# LLVM/LLD internals, reached only by builds configured WITH_LLVM=1. LLD in
# particular is documented as a deliberately arena-per-link, never-free
# design (it expects to run as a short-lived process), and LLVM's
# ManagedStatic registry is torn down by llvm_shutdown() only in binaries
# that call it. Neither is salam's memory to account for, and neither
# frame can appear in a stack that allocated something salam owns.
leak:llvm::
leak:lld::
